WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.430
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Ted Canova, who's an author practicing emersion

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:11.110
writing, as well as director of NY Use Arthur
L. Carter Journalism Institute.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:15.020
I've actually recommended one or two of Ted's
books to my audience in the past.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.230
Ted, I really appreciate your time.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:18.830
Thanks for talking to me.

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:24.029
So there are a lot of people in my audience
who may not immediately know what we mean

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:30.880
by immersion writing and some of the work
that you've done in advance, sometimes for

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.240
years before you will write a book.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:38.320
Certainly knew Jack where you actually became
a correctional officer in order to tell the

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.230
story of what that's like in coyotes.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:47.440
You actually travel across the US Mexico border
with undocumented immigrants and and even

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.800
subsequently work with them in multiple places
in the United States.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:57.210
So first, to just kind of frame it when you
start a project like this, what's the genesis

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.210
of an idea?

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:02.830
How do you think about your relationship to
the subject matter and start thinking about

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:04.900
how you'll carry the project out?

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:05.900
Sure.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:12.329
I guess you could say my approach is to try
to go more in-depth than a regular journalist.

00:01:12.329 --> 00:01:15.130
Can I look for a subject that matters to people?

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:17.219
Maybe it's mass incarceration.

00:01:17.219 --> 00:01:23.210
Maybe it's immigration, undocumented immigration
or homelessness.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:30.920
And I think, OK, what have journalists and
others been able to do to cast light on this

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:31.920
topic?

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:38.210
And what could I do if I were willing to spend
more time and get closer, get my hands dirty

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:41.630
if needed?

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:49.030
And often the answer has been, I try to go
beyond the sort of interview a journalist

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:56.180
does write, asking the five W's and all that
and spend time living with somebody else or

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:57.649
doing what they do.

00:01:57.649 --> 00:02:04.320
So my first book is about railroad tramps,
and I was still an undergraduate in college

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.240
and rode the rails.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:15.769
I met Mexicans while doing that and using
my high school Spanish, I realized they would

00:02:15.769 --> 00:02:17.180
talk to me.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:20.170
And in fact.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.819
It seemed open to it and it seemed open to
me hanging out.

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:27.540
And that became my my second book called Coyotes,
which you mentioned.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.349
And then, yeah, when I moved to New York from
Colorado.

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:39.409
Oh, gosh, 20 years ago, mass incarceration
was all over the news as it is again today.

00:02:39.409 --> 00:02:41.439
And.

00:02:41.439 --> 00:02:49.050
I thought, you know, there's got to be a way
to get closer to the subject than just.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:56.430
You know, calling up people on the phone or
doing interviews and ended up applying for

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:02.019
a job, as in New York state corrections officer,
which took about three years to get.

00:03:02.019 --> 00:03:03.120
And then I worked.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.330
That worked at SingSing almost a year for
that book.

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:09.970
And the idea is, yeah, I get closer.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:13.230
I'm not a standard journalist keeping my distance.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.379
I'm I'm right in there.

00:03:15.379 --> 00:03:18.530
And, you know, sometimes distance is good.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:19.900
It lets you be a little more objective.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.049
But I tend to get subjective.

00:03:22.049 --> 00:03:25.650
I say, you know, I'm a I'm a CEO.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:29.860
I'm scared of this situation or I'm a CEO.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:32.549
The guys around me are angry, and today I
understand it.

00:03:32.549 --> 00:03:33.900
I feel kind of angry myself.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:40.909
And and so I try to take notes on what it
feels like to be there.

00:03:40.909 --> 00:03:48.050
In the book, New Jack and also in some of
the research I did preparing for the interview.

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:55.290
You talk about the perception that could have
been the one that your your coworkers at SingSing

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:59.600
came away with when they learned about the
fact that you were researching and ultimately

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:03.390
wrote a book about your experience there,
that they might be mad at you.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:08.920
They might not like the fact that you essentially,
I mean, to call it it's not a ruse in the

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.680
sense that you really did go through the training.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:13.010
You really did work there.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:17.660
You nobody knew you weren't treated differently
because there was this book coming at the

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:23.300
end of the experience, but that maybe you
took advantage in some way that is not OK

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:28.440
or that they would in some way be displeased
with the fact that you were there, not just

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:33.400
as a correctional officer, but also as a writer
for sure.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:35.590
What was I mean.

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:43.050
Is there a fair critique to be made that you
were sort of using the resources of training

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:47.850
in order to end up writing a book, or do you
find that not to be a fair critique?

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:50.169
Well, you've asked a couple of questions.

00:04:50.169 --> 00:04:56.300
That last one is, did I, you know, somehow
take advantage of the resources of the state

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:02.910
of New York just to, you know, just to be
able to write something about it?

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:10.199
And in fact, I stayed on the job longer than
the typical trainee does.

00:05:10.199 --> 00:05:14.490
Most people drop out before I did and I stuck
with it.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:20.120
So I think New York State got their value
from me as a good employee.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:23.220
I got good job ratings.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:29.750
I tried really hard to be a good corrections
officer and understand what that meant.

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:38.020
That's one of two projects which you could
call undercover, because I could not tell

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:46.180
people my full background or my real agenda,
which was to write something about this.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:55.210
The other time I did that, I became a USDA
meat inspector at a beef plant in Nebraska.

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:57.350
And that was secretive, too.

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:05.780
And if you do something in a secretive way,
you are deceptive and deception is best avoided

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:07.560
in journalism.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:15.770
But there are times when in order to do something
important, I think deception is warranted.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:26.389
Not all journalists agree, but there was no
other way I could understand the life of an

00:06:26.389 --> 00:06:30.280
officer, a guard at Sing Sing without doing
that job.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:36.550
I had previously actually gotten an assignment
from The New Yorker to write about CEO's upstate

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:40.150
and then the state wouldn't let me go to work
with them.

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:44.180
They would let me visit a single prison once
for a short guided tour.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:47.669
But this is a big deal.

00:06:47.669 --> 00:06:53.539
It's Corrections is the second largest employer
in New York state or was when I was a CEO.

00:06:53.539 --> 00:06:57.340
And how could how do you justify keeping the
public out?

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:02.030
So I felt justified in getting the job secretly.

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:07.740
And then, you know, every journalist faces
a moment of reckoning when the when what they've

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:10.520
written comes out or what they've produced
on video.

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:14.669
The subjects like it or they don't.

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:18.659
And they say it's true or they say that's
B.S.

00:07:18.659 --> 00:07:24.440
It's not really different when you've done
something undercover people you write about

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:25.440
are still you.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:29.419
They're going to say that's B.S. or they're
going to say, yeah, well, actually, that's

00:07:29.419 --> 00:07:30.949
true.

00:07:30.949 --> 00:07:32.490
And.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:37.099
Far and away.

00:07:37.099 --> 00:07:39.850
CEOs said that book is true.

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:41.150
You know, it's warts and all.

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:43.860
We don't look good in the whole thing.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:50.490
But I got credit for doing the job myself
and and for telling the truth about it.

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:56.530
Most angry was the state of New York, which
put out a press release urging aggrieved employees

00:07:56.530 --> 00:08:02.610
to sue me if if they felt I had injured them
somehow.

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:11.099
And I think the people who felt injured, whether
the state corrections bureaucrats, who's who

00:08:11.099 --> 00:08:16.159
felt I'd snuck into their prisons and and
talked about it, which I wasn't supposed to

00:08:16.159 --> 00:08:17.340
do.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:21.690
One of the things in New Jack that comes out,
which I think couldn't possibly have come

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:29.099
out if you were just interviewing people,
is the sort of many sides of the prototypical

00:08:29.099 --> 00:08:35.070
correctional officer in the sense that there
is often conflict about their views of the

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:36.070
system.

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:43.280
There's conflict about the views on even individual
inmates as some of the stories that you tell.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:49.640
What else was only possible in the telling
of that story because you were immersed as

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:53.350
a correctional officer that by interviewing
people simply could not have been part of

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:54.350
the story.

00:08:54.350 --> 00:09:00.350
I'm really happy you brought up that example,
because to me, that is what this approach

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:08.170
lets me do, is look at a big fat stereotype
like the guard as bruit or the guard as sadist

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:10.660
and think and ask out loud.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:12.959
How much of that is true?

00:09:12.959 --> 00:09:14.110
And and you're right.

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:16.779
I found in some cases.

00:09:16.779 --> 00:09:18.190
Gosh.

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:23.560
That guy's pretty bad, but in lots of other
cases, in fact, in most cases what I found

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:26.240
was.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:33.269
Human beings trying to keep something good
in themselves alive while being immersed in

00:09:33.269 --> 00:09:34.730
this negative environment.

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:39.750
Day after day after day, like it's a crummy
job, it brings out the worst in them.

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:42.020
It brought out the worst in me.

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:49.770
My wife observed it, showed her my inner disciplinarian,
which I had actually never tried to cultivate.

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:53.660
But that's kind of what the job asked you
to do, is say no.

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:56.650
All day long to your fellow human beings.

00:09:56.650 --> 00:10:06.889
So the to get to your question, more particularly
working at Sing Sing, put me in a group of

00:10:06.889 --> 00:10:15.410
officers of color and there's no way I could
have learned the things I learned about their

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:16.410
lives.

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:17.410
Otherwise, I'm.

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:24.130
I remember the day I'm talking to the crew
I worked with in B Block.

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:27.100
It's this giant housing block, more than six
hundred prisoners.

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:31.670
We had like 15 minutes outside the mess hall
before we had to start frisking prisoners.

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:41.880
And a guy named Alcantara, who's Dominican
and lives near New York City.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:44.340
Describe double parking outside his mom's
building.

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:53.640
Because she was moving and he had his minivan
and he ran inside her lobby to pick something

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:55.320
up.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:11:01.899
And as he's running out, undercover officers
thinking he's part of a drug deal, tackle

00:11:01.899 --> 00:11:02.899
them.

00:11:02.899 --> 00:11:06.250
And I heard this, a version of this story
like six different times.

00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:08.550
These are officers of color.

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:13.810
I work with who are being pulled over by police
on traffic stops or they're being stopped

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:18.670
by undercovers in Harlem because they are
people of color.

00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:23.360
And that makes a big impression, you know.

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:28.390
And I wouldn't of been able to tell stories
like that had I not done the job myself.

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:33.519
One of the things that I took away from it
in hearing you describe in the book, kind

00:11:33.519 --> 00:11:38.889
of your personality transformation as you
describe and your wife dealing with it, is

00:11:38.889 --> 00:11:45.720
that maybe there really is more to this idea
that the focus on individuals in systems,

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:50.459
even now, like with the unrest and protests
that we are seeing, the focus on individuals

00:11:50.459 --> 00:11:53.720
is relevant because people are people as individuals.

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:57.519
But we shouldn't ignore the effect that systems
have.

00:11:57.519 --> 00:12:00.170
And that was a really big takeaway for me.

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:03.110
Yeah, the system makes you act a certain way.

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:11.899
Let's say you are a person convicted of a
crime who has had friends of different races.

00:12:11.899 --> 00:12:17.310
You know, you're from a place where it was
cool making friends who who didn't look like

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:18.310
you.

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:23.280
Well, you're going to prison and that's that
stuff doesn't fly in a lot of prisons.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:25.959
You have to stick with your own kind.

00:12:25.959 --> 00:12:31.709
You know, prisons are racially polarized.

00:12:31.709 --> 00:12:39.710
And then they're ideologically polarized and
they're organized by gang and and so prisoners,

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:42.180
inmates have to act a certain way.

00:12:42.180 --> 00:12:43.980
And then so do officers.

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:52.470
Let's say I'm an officer who thinks it's OK
to have a conversation with a prisoner.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:54.100
Like just, you know, I wish you did.

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:56.220
I mean, that was something you describe.

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:57.350
I thought, well, that's you.

00:12:57.350 --> 00:12:58.649
That's a good idea.

00:12:58.649 --> 00:12:59.649
Right?

00:12:59.649 --> 00:13:00.649
That's how you manage people.

00:13:00.649 --> 00:13:02.010
You talk to them.

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:06.459
There's a whole bunch of officers who who
think that's a terrible idea, that that you're

00:13:06.459 --> 00:13:10.300
going to you're going to get a reputation
as the inmates friend.

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:11.300
Right.

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:13.769
A guy who won't uphold the rules.

00:13:13.769 --> 00:13:14.769
And.

00:13:14.769 --> 00:13:20.570
And in fact, there were some cases like that
where some officers were just too friendly.

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:23.190
And you think, well, what's what's that about?

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:25.680
Because a certain distance is appropriate.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:35.510
But, yeah, you're in the system where your
uniform defines how you interact with other

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:36.560
people.

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:42.370
And and you're one of a group of people, whether
you're a prisoner or an officer, you're one

00:13:42.370 --> 00:13:43.830
of a group.

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:48.470
And you have to act in accordance with the
standards of that group.

00:13:48.470 --> 00:14:00.230
And gosh, when I see officers now standing
shoulder to shoulder to repel demonstrators,

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:05.389
I'm always thinking, OK, how many of them
are spoiling for a fight?

00:14:05.389 --> 00:14:07.170
Because you can tell some of them are right.

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:09.020
You know, all of their hearts are beating.

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:10.420
Bump, bump, bump.

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:12.540
They're like, OK, is it?

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:13.540
When's it gonna start?

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:14.959
How long can I restrain myself?

00:14:14.959 --> 00:14:20.009
What are who's going to stick up for me if
rock hits me in the head?

00:14:20.009 --> 00:14:22.610
But then, you know, there's a whole bunch
of them who are like, oh my God.

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:29.019
Let me just get home in one piece and back
to my family and my my toddler and.

00:14:29.019 --> 00:14:30.019
Right.

00:14:30.019 --> 00:14:33.850
I, I didn't really sign up to be in a riot.

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:37.540
And but how is how are those people going
to act when.

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:46.890
When 
it comes to violence, how will they?

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:50.240
Will they respond to their training with restraint?

00:14:50.240 --> 00:15:00.120
Will they respond to their, you know, that
their ancient brain and start firing a weapon

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:07.250
or flailing with a gun?

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:12.579
The whole question of how you act when you're
a law enforcement officer and part of a group

00:15:12.579 --> 00:15:19.339
that's under stress is superimportant and
matter so much today.

00:15:19.339 --> 00:15:25.610
And, yeah, so that's that's kind of the thing
I wanted to find out about.

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:29.750
Let's pause the conversation there on our
podcast, but we'll have the full conversation

00:15:29.750 --> 00:24:26.299
with Ted on our on our YouTube channel.

